const v10ReadA={id:"v10-read-a",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 69-72",title:"The Sinking Ground",description:"In parts of some coastal cities, the ground is slowly sinking, and in places the cause lies beneath people's feet. The process is called land subsidence, and one common cause is the heavy pumping of groundwater.\n\nUnderground, water fills the spaces between grains of sand and clay. When wells draw out more water than rain and rivers can replace, the water level falls. Layers of clay, no longer supported by the water in their pores, compress under the weight above them, and the surface settles. Unlike a sponge, compressed clay does not fully spring back when water returns, so much of the sinking is permanent.\n\nThe effects build up quietly. A few centimeters a year can seem trivial, but over decades it can leave streets lower than the high tide, so that flooding grows more frequent and drainage systems no longer flow as designed. Buildings and pipes may crack as the ground settles unevenly.\n\nThe main remedy is to reduce pumping, often by supplying piped surface water from rivers and dams so that homes and industries no longer depend on their own wells. Where this has been done, the sinking has slowed, though the ground that was lost is not regained.",alt:"Environmental science passage on land subsidence from groundwater pumping: clay compaction, why it is permanent, how it worsens flooding, and how piped surface water slows it."};
const v10ReadB={id:"v10-read-b",kind:"passage",label:"Babasahin para sa Aytem 73-76",title:"Bantay-Tubig sa Paaralan",description:"Isang malaking pampublikong paaralan ito na may ilang gusali, kantina, at gym. Binabayaran ng paaralan ang tubig mula sa limitadong pondo, kaya bawat pagtaas ng bayarin ay nangangahulugang mas kaunti ang pondo para sa ibang pangangailangan.\n\nNapansin ng isang paaralan na mataas ang konsumo ng tubig kahit walang klase tuwing Sabado.\n\nInakala ng ilang guro na may gumagamit ng tubig nang walang pahintulot tuwing Sabado. Ang iba naman ay nagsabing baka mali lamang ang pagbasa sa metro.\n\nSinuri ng pangkat ang buwanang metro ngunit hindi nito matukoy kung aling gusali ang may suliranin. Naglagay sila ng pansamantalang metro sa bawat gusali sa loob ng dalawang linggo. Lumitaw na tuloy-tuloy ang agos sa lumang palikuran malapit sa gym. Matapos palitan ang sirang balbula, bumaba ang konsumo sa katapusan ng linggo. Iminungkahi ng pangkat na ipagpatuloy ang hiwa-hiwalay na pagsukat kada quarter upang maagang makita ang mga tagas.",alt:"Teksto tungkol sa paaralang gumamit ng pansamantalang metro sa bawat gusali, natukoy ang sirang balbula sa lumang palikuran, at nagmungkahing ulitin ang hiwa-hiwalay na pagsukat kada quarter."};
const v10ReadC={id:"v10-read-c",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 77-80",title:"Why the Sky Is Blue",description:"Sunlight looks white, but it is a mixture of all the colors of the rainbow, each with a different wavelength. Red light has the longest wavelengths that we can see, and violet and blue have the shortest.\n\nWhen sunlight enters the atmosphere, it meets countless molecules of nitrogen and oxygen. These molecules are far smaller than the wavelengths of visible light, and they scatter shorter wavelengths much more strongly than longer ones. Blue light is therefore bounced in every direction across the sky, while most red and yellow light continues along its original path. Wherever we look, away from the sun, some of that scattered blue light reaches our eyes.\n\nViolet light is scattered even more than blue, so why is the sky not violet? Part of the reason is that sunlight contains less violet than blue, and part is that our eyes are more sensitive to blue.\n\nAt sunrise and sunset, sunlight must travel through much more air to reach us. By the time it arrives, most of the blue has been scattered away along the route, leaving the reds and oranges that color the evening sky.",alt:"Science passage on how air molecules scatter short wavelengths, why the sky is blue rather than violet, and why sunsets are red and orange."};
